<div class="jr-panel"><div class="jr-narr"><p class="jr-count">Step 1 of 9</p><h3 class="jr-h">Run the build</h3><p class="jr-body">In the project <code>vorpal init</code> makes for Python, you type <code>vorpal build example</code>. The CLI reads <code>Vorpal.toml</code>, builds your config program from <code>src/vorpal.py</code> (the config program is an artifact too) and starts it.</p><p class="jr-ref">In the code: cli/src/command/build.rs · build_config_binary</p><ul class="jr-rows" aria-label="Artifacts at this step"></ul></div>
<fieldset class="jr-scn"><legend>What if…</legend><label><input type="radio" name="j1-scn" value="first" checked><span>First build</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="j1-scn" value="again"><span>Build again, nothing changed</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="j1-scn" value="edit"><span>Edit src/main.py</span></label><label><input type="radio" name="j1-scn" value="ci"><span>Second machine or CI</span></label></fieldset></div>
<div class="jr-dig"><p class="jr-strip"></p><p class="jr-note">Digests are computed in your browser from this example definition (SHA-256).</p>
<details class="jr-more"><summary>See exactly what is hashed</summary><div class="jr-defs"></div></details></div>
<details class="jr-text" open data-jr-text><summary>Read the journey as text</summary><ol class="jr-steps"><li><b>Run the build.</b> In the project <code>vorpal init</code> makes for Python, you type <code>vorpal build example</code>. The CLI reads <code>Vorpal.toml</code>, builds your config program from <code>src/vorpal.py</code> (the config program is an artifact too) and starts it. <i>Build again, nothing changed:</i> Same command, nothing edited. The config program is already in the store, so the CLI starts it straight away. <i>Edit src/main.py:</i> You changed one line in <code>src/main.py</code>. The template’s <code>Vorpal.toml</code> lists <code>src</code> among the config program’s own sources, so the config program gets a new digest and is rebuilt before it starts. <i>Second machine or CI:</i> A teammate or a CI job on the same system (here <code>aarch64-linux</code>) checks out the same commit, <code>Vorpal.lock</code> included, and runs the same command. For a pull to work, both machines must use one remote registry (<code>vorpal config set registry &lt;address></code>). By default each machine uses its own local registry. <span class="jr-ref">In the code: cli/src/command/build.rs · build_config_binary</span></li><li><b>Declare the artifacts.</b> The config program runs. <code>Python("example", SYSTEMS)</code> declares the app, and the Python builder also declares the tools it needs: <code>cpython</code> and <code>uv</code>. <code>example</code>’s build step names both tools, so it depends on them. Each declaration goes to the agent before it gets a digest. <span class="jr-ref">In the code: sdk/python/…/language/python.py · Python.build</span></li><li><b>Pin the sources.</b> <code>cpython</code> and <code>uv</code> come from <code>sdk.vorpal.build</code>. Their digests are already pinned in <code>Vorpal.lock</code> (written earlier by <code>vorpal prepare</code>). The agent downloads each archive once, checks it against its pin (a mismatch stops the build unless you pass <code>--unlock</code>) and pushes it to the registry. <code>example</code>’s source is local: the agent hashes <code>src/</code> and the other includes now, after setting every file time to 1970, so only content counts. <i>Build again, nothing changed:</i> The pins in <code>Vorpal.lock</code> match and the registry already holds those source archives, so nothing is downloaded. The agent hashes the local <code>src/</code> again: same content, same digest. <i>Edit src/main.py:</i> The pins are unchanged, so nothing is downloaded. Local sources are not pinned in <code>Vorpal.lock</code>: the agent hashes them on every build, and your edit gives <code>example</code>’s source a new digest. <i>Second machine or CI:</i> The checkout carries the same <code>Vorpal.lock</code>, and the shared registry already holds the pinned source archives, so machine B downloads nothing. Its <code>src/</code> hashes to the same digest as on machine A. <span class="jr-ref">In the code: cli/src/command/start/agent.rs · hydrate_and_gate_source</span></li><li><b>Give every definition a digest.</b> The agent serialises each prepared definition (target, sources with their digests, steps, systems, aliases, name) to JSON and takes its SHA-256. That digest names the artifact. <code>example</code>’s step lists the <code>cpython</code> and <code>uv</code> digests, so a new tool version would change <code>example</code> too. <i>Build again, nothing changed:</i> Every input is the same as last time, so every digest is the same as last time. <i>Edit src/main.py:</i> Only <code>example</code> changes, because its source digest moved. <code>cpython</code> and <code>uv</code> keep their digests. <i>Second machine or CI:</i> Machine B computes the same three digests as machine A: same definitions, same system. <span class="jr-ref">In the code: cli/src/command/start/agent.rs · prepare_artifact</span></li><li><b>Look in the local store.</b> For each artifact in dependency order, the CLI checks <code>/var/lib/vorpal/store/artifact/output/&lt;namespace>/&lt;digest></code>. The store is empty, so all three are missing. <i>Build again, nothing changed:</i> All three digests are in the store. Vorpal skips them: no registry call, no build. The rest of this journey is skipped. <i>Edit src/main.py:</i> <code>cpython</code> and <code>uv</code> are found and skipped. The new <code>example</code> digest is not there. The old one still is: Vorpal keeps outputs until you run <code>vorpal system prune</code>. <i>Second machine or CI:</i> Machine B’s store is empty, so all three are missing locally. <span class="jr-ref">In the code: cli/src/command/build.rs · build</span></li><li><b>Ask the registry.</b> For each miss, the CLI asks the registry (local by default) for <code>&lt;digest>.tar.zst</code>. The registry holds the source archives but no built outputs yet, so all three miss. <i>Build again, nothing changed:</i> Skipped. Everything was already in the local store. <i>Edit src/main.py:</i> The new <code>example</code> digest has never been built anywhere, so the registry misses too. <i>Second machine or CI:</i> This is the remote registry both machines use, and machine A’s builds pushed all three archives here. Machine B pulls them and unpacks them into its own store. Nothing is built. <span class="jr-ref">In the code: cli/src/command/build.rs · pull_archive</span></li><li><b>Build what is missing.</b> The worker builds each miss in dependency order: <code>cpython</code>, <code>uv</code>, then <code>example</code>. It pulls sources and dependency outputs from the registry into a fresh workspace and runs the steps. Steps run in a sandbox: on Linux, bubblewrap on the <code>linux-vorpal</code> root filesystem (macOS sandboxing is planned: ENG-364). It packs the output with zstd and pushes it to the registry. <i>Build again, nothing changed:</i> Idle. There is nothing to build. <i>Edit src/main.py:</i> Only <code>example</code> is built. The worker pulls the <code>cpython</code> and <code>uv</code> outputs as they are, runs <code>example</code>’s steps in a fresh workspace and pushes the new archive to the registry. <i>Second machine or CI:</i> Idle. Machine B builds nothing. <span class="jr-ref">In the code: cli/src/command/start/worker.rs · build_artifact</span></li><li><b>Land in the store.</b> The CLI pulls each new archive back from the registry, unpacks it into the local store and prints <code>example</code>’s digest. Add <code>--path</code> to print the output path instead. <i>Build again, nothing changed:</i> Nothing moved. The CLI prints the same digest as last time. <i>Edit src/main.py:</i> The CLI pulls the new <code>example</code> archive back, unpacks it next to the old one and prints the new digest. <i>Second machine or CI:</i> Already unpacked in the registry step. The CLI prints the same <code>example</code> digest that machine A printed. <span class="jr-ref">In the code: cli/src/command/build.rs · unpack_archive_if_present</span></li><li><b>Run it.</b> <code>vorpal run example</code> resolves the alias <code>example</code> to a digest (local alias first, then the registry), finds that output (pulling it from the registry if it is missing) and runs its <code>bin/example</code>. The alias comes from one line added to the template: <code>.with_aliases(["example"])</code>. <i>Second machine:</i> <code>vorpal run example</code> on machine B resolves the alias and runs <code>bin/example</code> from its own store. B could even skip the build: <code>vorpal run</code> looks the alias up in the registry and pulls the output itself. <span class="jr-ref">In the code: cli/src/command/run.rs · resolve_alias_digest</span></li></ol><table class="jr-tab"><caption>What happens to each artifact</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Situation</th><th scope="col">cpython</th><th scope="col">uv</th><th scope="col">example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">First build</th><td>built by the worker</td><td>built by the worker</td><td>built by the worker</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Build again, nothing changed</th><td>found in store</td><td>found in store</td><td>found in store</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Edit src/main.py</th><td>found in store</td><td>found in store</td><td>new digest, built by the worker</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Second machine or CI</th><td>pulled from registry</td><td>pulled from registry</td><td>pulled from registry</td></tr></tbody></table></details>
<script is:inline>document.documentElement.classList.contains("vp-js")&&document.querySelector("[data-jr-text]").removeAttribute("open")</script>
</section>

<script>
	const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-journey]');
	if (root) {
		let started: Promise<void> | undefined;
		let pending: HTMLElement | null = null;
		const start = () =>
			(started ??= import('../scripts/journey.js').then(({ mountJourney }) => {
				mountJourney(root);
				const el = pending;
				pending = null;
				el?.click();
			}));
		const early = (event: Event) => {
			if (event.type === 'click' && !root.dataset.jrReady) {
				const target = (event.target as Element).closest<HTMLElement>('button');
				if (target) {
					event.preventDefault();
					pending = target;
				}
			}
			start();
		};
		for (const type of ['pointerdown', 'focusin', 'keydown', 'click']) root.addEventListener(type, early, { capture: true });
		if ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {
			const io = new IntersectionObserver(
				(entries) => {
					if (entries.some((e) => e.isIntersecting)) {
						io.disconnect();
						start();
					}
				},
				{ rootMargin: '300px' },
			);
			io.observe(root);
		} else start();
	}
</script>
